-- ------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Seven Days Later: Ransomware Across the Estate

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------

insert into scenarios (
  slug, title, category, difficulty, estimated_minutes, summary, briefing_md,
  learning_objectives, organization_name, root_cause, model_recommendations,
  continues_from, is_published
) values (
  'ransomware-outbreak', 'Seven Days Later: Ransomware Across the Estate', 'ransomware'::scenario_category,
  'advanced'::scenario_difficulty, 75,
  'At 07:41 five systems begin encrypting themselves within eight minutes of each other. The trail leads back to an incident this SOC closed a week ago without rebuilding the host.', '## Your shift

You are the on-call analyst. It is 07:52 on a Tuesday and your phone has been
going for eleven minutes.

## What is happening right now

At **07:41:12** the endpoint agent on `WKS-HR-014` reported 1,842 files
renamed to a `.nocturnelock` extension in sixty seconds. By the time you open
the console, the same detection has fired on four more systems, including
**FS-SHARED-01**, the departmental file server.

This is not an investigation into something that happened. It is happening.

## What you need to do

Contain it first and understand it second &mdash; the opposite order to every
other scenario in this library, and a genuinely different discipline. Then
work out how it started, because the answer is uncomfortable and it is the
reason this scenario exists.

You will find that this SOC has seen the first stage of this attack before.

> This scenario continues *Payroll Pressure: Phishing to Endpoint
> Compromise*. You can work it alone, but running them back to back shows how
> one incomplete remediation becomes an estate-wide event.

> All data in this scenario is synthetic. Addresses use RFC 5737
> documentation ranges and domains use RFC 2606 reserved TLDs; none of it
> resolves or routes anywhere.
',
  array['Respond under time pressure when the damage is already occurring.', 'Recognise pre-encryption behaviour - shadow copy deletion, service stops - as the last warning before impact.', 'Understand how a mapped drive spreads encryption without the malware moving host.', 'Judge whether a backup is genuinely usable, not merely present.', 'Sequence recovery so that restored systems are not immediately re-encrypted.', 'Confront the cost of closing an incident without eradicating it.']::text[], 'Northwind Polytechnic',
  'Incident INC-2025-0311 was closed on 11 March after blocking the command-and-control domain at the web proxy, but the affected workstation was never isolated or rebuilt, the Run-key persistence was never removed, the user''s credentials were never reset, and the domain was never blocked at DNS or the firewall. The implant remained on WKS-HR-014 for seven days. On 18 March it launched at logon, resolved the same C2 domain through a path that had not been blocked, downloaded a ransomware payload, deleted shadow copies, stopped backup and database services, and encrypted the workstation and every share it had a mapped drive to.', array['Require that any host with confirmed code execution is rebuilt from a known-good image before it returns to service, with an explicit named exception process rather than an informal one.', 'Block confirmed C2 indicators at every layer - DNS, proxy and firewall - and verify the block from the affected host rather than assuming it applied.', 'Define closure criteria for incidents that include eradication of persistence and credential rotation, so a ticket cannot be closed while the implant is still resident.', 'Restrict write access to departmental shares so a single compromised workstation cannot encrypt 42,000 files over a mapped drive.', 'Keep backups on a separate network with separate credentials, and restore-test a full system at least twice a year rather than testing single files.', 'Run a threat hunt for the original indicators across the whole estate after any confirmed compromise, rather than treating the detected host as the whole incident.']::text[],
  'phishing-initial-access', true
)
on conflict (slug) do update set
